    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. specifically with the infection part, what are the red flags for an internal abscess or something similar developing inside the rumen? the incision looks okay from the outside but i'm worried about stuff brewing inside where i can't see it.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For internal abscesses or similar complications forming within the rumen after a rumenotomy, be vigilant for signs such as persistently lethargic behavior, an appetite reduced from her usual level (which could hint at pain or discomfort), signs of colic or straining without apparent cause, sudden changes in temperature or erratic heat cycles, and evidence of discharge from the surgical site. If you notice any of these symptoms, consult a veterinarian immediately as internal abscesses might require medical intervention. Additionally, reassess the incision regularly for proper healing, and perform any recommended diagnostic procedures such as ultrasound or fine-needle aspiration, as ordered by your veterinarian, for suspected internal abscesses that are not visible externally.",
